<https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/evidence-wtfia-2009> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Sun & Rain Works holds the WTFIA folder: the working source material it assembled for its website www.wtfisart.com, comprising digital files dated 2008 to 2009. As held, the folder contains four Microsoft Word documents together with associated sets of reference images. The documents are: 'IsItArt.doc', a fourteen-page compilation of roughly four thousand words of excerpts and source links drawn from art-market press published between 2007 and 2009, covering museum deaccessioning, the contraction of the auction market, art-focused hedge funds, and art treated as an asset class, with material attributed to The New York Times, The Daily Telegraph, the Financial Times, The Nation, The Guardian, Deutsche Welle, CathNews, Livemint, The New York Sun, Portfolio.com, and the Los Angeles Times; 'Photographers.doc', a one-page biographical note on the photographer Lee Balterman; and two dealer-network directories built as the site's link content, 'OldMasters.doc', an eight-page directory of Old Master dealers and galleries with their web addresses, and 'Modern.doc', a directory of modern and contemporary galleries in New York, Florida, and California. The accompanying images comprise seventeen art-market and art-trade pictures dated 2009-03-23, showing auction houses, collectors, art fairs, and finance motifs; sixteen art-historical reference plates dated between 2009-01-21 and 2009-01-26; two paintings of the cleansing of the temple dated 2009-01-22; and thirty photographic files dated between 2008-08-07 and 2009-01-21. Together these evidence the material Sun & Rain Works assembled for www.wtfisart.com, and a dated 2008-2009 compilation activity concerned with the art market, the dealer network, and art history. The folder holds no HTML, so it does not by itself record what the website published or when it went live, and it establishes no precedence over any later artist, work, or event." .
