_:r11b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: public postings of Metaconceptual Art (2011-2012)" .
_:r11b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r11b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r11b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r11b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-pastebin-2011-2012" .
_:r11b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r11b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r11b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: publicly verifiable on a third-party platform, and the earliest such evidence in this collection. The postings are readable by anyone at https://pastebin.com/AmQ3P87v and https://pastebin.com/18ndp2CF ; both are set never to expire. Their dates are assigned by the platform at the moment of posting and are carried in the page markup as 'Thursday 22nd of November 2012 11:19:57 PM CDT' and 'Monday 28th of November 2011 07:51:51 AM CDT'. Unlike a profile biography, the body of a paste is fixed once posted. The platform also records public circulation: 950 views for the 2012 posting and 433 for the 2011 one at the time of compilation. Nothing here is studio-held, so verification requires no access to the private archive." .
_:r11b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Relation to the published Eight Sentences. The 2012 posting is the earliest located instance of the sentence sequence and predates its publication on this site by fourteen years. Its wording is the wording published here, allowing for three trivial variances: 'commodification of Art, and its relationship' has since lost its comma, 'the Art World , and Art History' has lost a space, and 'Transcendance' has been corrected to 'Transcendence'. The persistence of a misspelling across the interval, and its later silent normalisation, are recorded because they are the sort of detail by which a text's descent is traced. The sequence's stated debt to Sol LeWitt's 'Sentences on Conceptual Art' is not present in the 2012 posting and appears to be a later framing." .
_:r11b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Limits. Both postings are the artist's own; the platform dates when he said something publicly, not whether what he said was true. They therefore evidence the public existence of the term and its definition at those dates, and nothing about the 2009 auction, which neither posting mentions. The 2011 posting describes its poem as written 'a few years ago', which would place composition somewhere in the late 2000s; that is the artist's own statement, made in 2011 rather than in retrospect from the present, and it narrows but does not close the gap to the asserted 2007 origin. The origin date itself remains unevidenced. Both postings link to a Sun & Rain Works page on Facebook. That page has since been deleted by the studio, and the Internet Archive holds no capture of it, so it cannot be examined and any earlier material it carried is lost. Its former existence is not in doubt: the 2012 posting cites its address and presents the eight characteristics as the page's content, which places the definition on that page on or before 2012-11-22. The postings recorded here are consequently the surviving witness to a source that no longer exists, and they are load-bearing for that reason." .
_:r11b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Preservation note. Of the sources that once carried this material, several no longer exist: the Facebook page has been deleted, the YouTube videos cited in the 2013 correspondence are gone, and the mailbox from which that correspondence was sent has been closed. What survives does so on a third-party platform the studio does not control, in two postings set never to expire. Their continued availability is not guaranteed by anyone. The studio has therefore taken a local copy of both postings - the paste bodies and the rendered pages, the latter retained because they carry the platform's own timestamp string that the raw endpoint omits - published alongside this record with checksums at data/evidence/pastebin-2011-2012/. Those files are a preservation copy and not the dated source: a local file proves what was retrieved and when it was retrieved, and nothing more. The dating rests on Pastebin's records, which assign a posting timestamp the poster cannot alter, and should be verified against the live URLs for as long as they resolve." .
_:r11b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r11b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r11b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r11b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r11b8 .
_:r11b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r11b7 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2011-2012 public-posting evidence record" .
_:r11b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r11b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r11b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r11b8 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r12b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: Sun and Rain Works in a market's membership records (2005, 2006, 2008)" .
_:r12b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r12b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r12b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r12b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-saturday-market-membership" .
_:r12b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r12b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r12b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: an institution speaking from its own records, and the earliest such statement in this collection. Its weight comes from three things. The market is not the studio and has no stake in the studio's claims. The years it gives are drawn from membership records it keeps for its own purposes, not from anything the studio supplied to it. And the statement is re-checkable: the market still exists, still holds the records, and can be asked again by anyone. Against that, one limit must be stated. The reply is held only as a screenshot of a mail client; the message itself has not been examined, no headers or checksum are published, and the exchange is therefore reported rather than verified in the way the 2013 museum correspondence has been. Obtaining the message file, or a written statement on the market's letterhead, would close that gap." .
_:r12b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What it establishes, and what it does not. It establishes that a business trading as Sun and Rain Works existed and was selling work in Eugene, Oregon in 2005, six years before the earliest public use of the term Metaconceptual Art and three years before the studio's first documented approach to an art institution about the Adams photograph. It removes a question that would otherwise sit under every earlier claim in this collection, which is whether the studio existed as a working art business at the dates it describes. It does not touch Metaconceptual Art at all. A market's membership roll records who paid to sell at its stalls; it says nothing about what anyone thought, wrote, or made, and no part of it evidences the 2007 origin this collection asserts elsewhere. The market's records also say nothing about who held the membership beyond the business name." .
_:r12b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A coincidence, recorded and not interpreted. The years given are 2005, 2006 and 2008. The one year missing from that run is 2007, which is the year this collection asserts as the origin of Metaconceptual Art. Nothing is drawn from this. Market memberships lapse for ordinary reasons - a season away, a change of circumstances, a year not trading - and no document connects the gap to the assertion. It is noted here because a reader comparing the two dates will notice it, and because a record that omitted it would be less honest than one that does not." .
_:r12b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction. Following this collection's convention, the market's general manager, who answered the inquiry in an official capacity, is identified by role only; their name, direct mailbox and telephone number are withheld pending consent and retained in the studio's private copy. The organisation itself is named, because the identity of the attesting body is what gives the statement its weight. No endorsement by the Eugene Saturday Market of this project, of Metaconceptual Art, or of any claim in this collection is implied or should be inferred: the market answered a factual question about its own membership records and nothing more." .
_:r12b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r12b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r12b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r12b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r12b7 .
_:r12b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r12b6 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the market membership record" .
_:r12b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r12b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r12b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r12b7 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r13b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the approach to a museum photography department (11 August 2008)" .
_:r13b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r13b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r13b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r13b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-sfmoma-approach-2008" .
_:r13b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r13b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r13b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two errors in the letter, recorded because they are evidence of the kind of document it is. The writer dates the photograph to 'what appears to be 1946'. The Sotheby's catalogue published seven months later gives 1952, printed probably in the 1950s. An earlier version of this record cited a shortcut file in the studio's archive, whose name carries the year 1946, as contemporaneous corroboration of that belief. That was wrong and is withdrawn: the shortcut was created on 2026-08-20 and points to a file on the present machine, so it records a recent naming choice and evidences nothing about 2008. The 1946 belief is attested by this letter alone, which is sufficient for the purpose - the letter is contemporaneous and states the belief in the writer's own words - and was later corrected by the auction house's cataloguing. Second, the letter reports contact with a named person at 'the Ansel Adams Musem in SanFrancisco'. No institution of that name was operating in San Francisco in 2008: the Ansel Adams Center for Photography, run by the Friends of Photography, closed permanently in 2001 and the organisation dissolved. The writer may have meant a differently named body, or a person formerly associated with that one. Neither error is material to anything this collection claims, and both are the ordinary imprecision of a person writing in real time rather than the tidiness of a document assembled afterwards." .
_:r13b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and scope. The individual named in the letter as a contact at an Adams institution is not named here; that name is withheld pending consent, following this collection's convention, and is retained in the studio's private copy, as are the writer's then-current mailbox and telephone number. The recipient institution is named, because the identity of the body approached is what gives the approach its meaning. No interest, response, endorsement, or acquisition by the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art is claimed or should be inferred: this record evidences an unanswered letter. It establishes nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection, and nothing about the auction, which had not been proposed when it was written." .
_:r13b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The artist's account of the word, recorded as his account and given in 2026. Sun & Rain Works states that the work was made under the influence of the Happenings of the 1960s, and that the word 'happening' in this letter was used in that sense deliberately. The statement differs from the studio's account of the concealment, recorded in the correspondence record, in one respect that matters: that account explained an absence, why nothing was said to the auction house, and could not be checked against anything. This one makes a claim about a specific word in a specific surviving document, and can therefore be set beside it. Two features of the letter are consistent with the account. The recipient is the photography department of a museum of modern art, which is the institutional home of that tradition rather than an incidental choice of correspondent. And the phrasing is marked: a writer meaning only an occurrence would more naturally have offered the museum the discovery or the photograph, whereas this sentence offers 'this happening' as the thing that might interest them, which is the situation rather than the object." .
_:r13b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Why that account cannot settle the question it addresses, stated plainly because it is the central difficulty in this collection. What is at issue is whether a framing was present in 2008 or arrived later. An artist's testimony given in 2026 about what he meant in 2008 is not independent of that question: it is the thing being asked about. Memory reconstructs, and it does so sincerely. A person who came to understand an ambiguous choice as deliberate some years after making it will report, honestly and without any intention to mislead, that it was deliberate at the time. Nothing in this record suggests that is what happened here; the point is that this record cannot distinguish it from the alternative, and neither can the testimony, because the two produce identical statements. The account is therefore recorded, weighed as consistent with the document, and treated as carrying little evidential weight of its own. Only something written before the framing was in question could carry more." .
_:r13b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A relevant negative from the working archive. The studio's source folder for www.wtfisart.com, ninety-eight files assembled between August 2008 and March 2009 and searched exhaustively in 2026, contains no occurrence of the word happening, none of performance, and none of conceptual art. Its content is art-market press, dealer directories and art-historical reference plates. If the Happenings of the 1960s were the operative influence in that period, the folder carries no trace of that particular lineage. One qualification belongs here, and it cuts the other way. The folder is not empty of the studio's stated concerns: 'IsItArt.doc' compiles, among its twenty-four press reports, an article quoting Andy Warhol's dictum that good business is the best art - the central idea of the volume the studio had composed into its documentation photographs five months earlier. So a conceptual lineage is present in the contemporaneous papers; it is Warhol and art-as-commerce rather than Kaprow and the Happening. This should not be pressed too far in either direction: the folder was assembled for a website about the art market, and a person does not research what he already knows. But an influence claimed for this period leaves no mark in the only substantial body of the studio's own contemporaneous papers that survives, and a record that omitted that would be selecting its evidence." .
_:r13b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r13b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r13b16 .
_:r13b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r13b15 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the museum approach record" .
_:r13b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r13b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r13b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r13b16 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r13b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: held as a screenshot of a mail client, and therefore reported rather than verified. No message file has been examined, no headers or Message-ID inspected, and no checksum is published. The screenshot shows a quoted header block giving sender, recipient, a send time of Monday, August 11, 2008 at 4:01 PM, and the subject. The recipient is an institutional mailbox at a museum which may hold its own copy. The studio put a records request to the museum on 2026-09-01 asking whether its holdings reach this message; no reply had been received when this record was written. If the museum produces a copy, this record moves from reported to institutionally retained, as the 2013 correspondence did, and should be revised accordingly. If the museum searches and finds nothing, that negative finding should be recorded here with its scope stated, as the 2009 negative finding is recorded in the museum correspondence record. No reply from the museum is held, and nothing in this collection shows that the museum answered, acknowledged, or acted on the message. What is evidenced is that the approach was made, not that it was received by any particular person or had any effect." .
_:r13b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A second view of the same message, added 2026-09-10. A further screenshot shows the message itself, open in the sent mail of the same mailbox rather than quoted inside a forward: the same sender, recipient, subject, body and closing, the attachment 'San Francisco.zip' at 3 MB, and a banner recording that the studio forwarded the message on 2024-09-03. Two things follow. First, the original is still held in a live mailbox, so it can be exported as a message file with its full headers and Message-ID, which is the step that would move this record from reported to verified. Second, this view displays the send time as 3:01 PM, an hour earlier than the 4:01 PM of the quoted header block. The raw Date line of the sibling message described below, 'Mon, 11 Aug 2008 16:01:02 -0700', is 23:01 UTC, which a client displays as 4:01 PM at UTC minus seven and as 3:01 PM at UTC minus eight. The difference is therefore the client's display setting, not a second send time: this view renders times at UTC minus eight, although the date falls in Pacific daylight time. A display setting describes the viewer, not the sender, so it says nothing about where the message was written. The calibration is useful beyond this record, because the consignment agreement record has a timestamp that turns on the same client's offset. The 2024 forward is not otherwise documented in this collection." .
_:r13b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "It moves two dates. Until this message was produced, the earliest dated instance of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection was the approach to Sotheby's of 2008-08-14, and the earliest dated event in the auction engagement was the documentation session of 2008-08-08. This letter is dated 2008-08-11, so the name and the self-description as an artist and sole proprietor are now attested three days earlier, and the first documented outreach about the photograph was to a museum rather than to the trade. The order matters and is worth stating plainly: the museum was written to first, the auction house second. It is not a large interval, and both fall in the same week as the photographs." .
_:r13b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "A sibling message of the same hour, and the first transmission header in this part of the collection. A further screenshot shows a message the studio forwarded on 2008-08-14 at 12:27 to a photographic-services address, carrying the same attachment, 'San Francisco.zip' at 3 MB. What it quotes beneath is not a rendered client view but a raw header block: sender, the subject 'Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco', and 'Date: Mon, 11 Aug 2008 16:01:02 -0700'. Three things in that line check out independently. 11 August 2008 was in fact a Monday. The offset of seven hours behind UTC is Pacific Daylight Time, which is what Oregon was observing in August, agreeing with the Eugene address the letters give. And the clock time of 16:01 is the same minute the museum letter's client view reports. The header is displayed rather than verified from a message file, so this is a stronger class of screenshot rather than a different class of evidence; but a raw Date line with a correct weekday and a correct seasonal offset is materially better than a client's rendering of a date." .
_:r13b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "That message is not the museum letter, and the difference should not be glossed. The museum letter opens 'Dear Sir or Madame'; this one opens with the first name of a named individual, and its body is shorter, omitting the paragraphs about ownership, research, the Portland appraisal contacts and the Adams institution. So the studio was sending the same subject line and the same attachment to more than one party within the same minute or two of the same afternoon, varying the salutation and the length. The header therefore corroborates the date, the hour and the timezone of the museum letter without being that letter's own header. Obtaining the museum letter as a message file remains the thing that would verify it directly." .
_:r13b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The word 'happening', and how much weight it can bear. The message says: 'I thought that this happening might be of some interest to SF MOMA.' Two readings are available and this record does not choose between them. On the first, 'happening' is the ordinary English noun for an event or occurrence, and the sentence means no more than that the writer thought the situation might interest the museum. On the second, 'happening' is the term of art for a constructed, participatory art event, associated with Allan Kaprow and current in exactly the institutional context being written to, and the sentence offers the situation itself, rather than the object, as the thing of interest. The second reading is available because of who was being addressed: a modern art museum's photography department, by a writer who has just called himself an artist. It is not compelled, because the sentence works perfectly well under the first reading and nothing else in the message develops the idea. This is the closest thing to a contemporaneous conceptual framing of the situation that this collection holds, and it is a single ambiguous word in a letter otherwise concerned with attribution, appraisal and significance." .
_:r13b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The competing reading of the week as a whole, which should be weighed against the above. Within a few days in August 2008 the writer photographed the print, approached a museum, approached an auction house, sent the same letter and attachment to at least one further named individual and onward to a photographic-services address, reported contact with appraisers on the Portland art scene, and reported contact with a named person at an Adams institution. That is five or more parties in one week. That is the pattern of someone who believes he has found something valuable and is establishing what it is and what it is worth. The stated purpose in this letter is research into historical and artistic significance, and the appraisal contacts are about value. On that reading the approach to the museum is a request for expertise, which is an ordinary thing to ask a museum for, and carries no artistic intention at all. The breadth of the outreach supports this reading at least as well as the conceptual one, and a record that cited the word 'happening' without citing the surrounding behaviour would be selecting its evidence." .
_:r13b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r13b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Where the letter went. The photograph is of San Francisco, the subject line places the finding there ('Ansel Adams Finds Art in SanFrancisco'), and the letter went to the modern art museum of that same city. Sun & Rain Works treats this as part of a deliberate framing, and the letter is consistent with that account. It does not discriminate between the two readings above, however: a person researching an Adams photograph of San Francisco would have had every reason to write to that city's museum of modern art first. What it does establish is narrower. The approach was addressed and composed rather than incidental - a chosen institution, a written headline, the same text sent to several parties within the week - which is evidence of deliberate outreach. It is not evidence of an intention, in 2008, to make the outreach an artwork, and should not be read as more." .
_:r13b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r13b9 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence archive: the Sotheby's consignment correspondence (2008-2009)" .
_:r14b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300404670> .
_:r14b0 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P72_has_language> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300388277> .
_:r14b0 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_E41_Linguistic_Appellation> .
_:r14b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "record:evidence-sothebys-approach-2008" .
_:r14b1 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300312355> .
_:r14b1 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E42_Identifier> .
_:r14b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Four independent agreements between the message and the photographs, each checkable. The message describes the attached photographs as made with a 1.3 megapixel FujiFilm camera; the frames carry camera-written Exif naming a FUJIFILM FinePix1300 and measure 1280 by 960 pixels, which is 1.23 megapixels, the rating the model is sold under. The message shows an attachment of 3 MB; the eleven frames and the thumbnail cache occupy 3,553,357 bytes, which a mail client displays as 3 MB. The message is dated 2008-08-14; the frames are dated 2008-08-08, six days earlier. The attachment is named for San Francisco; the lot is titled 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks'. This is the first point in the collection at which camera-written metadata on a studio-held file is corroborated by a separate document rather than only by itself. The corroboration is not independent of the studio, since both artifacts come from it, but it is independent of the files: the caveat that Exif can in principle be altered would now require the message to have been fabricated to match it." .
_:r14b10 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b10 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the archive cannot do. A zip file carries the timestamps of the files put into it, not a record of when it was made, so this one cannot date its own creation and does not by itself establish that anything was sent in 2008. Its container fingerprint is worth recording because it is unexpected: create-system 3 with Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields means the archive was written by Info-ZIP tooling on a Unix-like system, not by the Windows compressed-folder command, although the thumbnail cache inside it shows the source folder was a Windows one. Whether that reflects the machine used in 2008 or a later repackaging is not determinable from the file. The honest position is that the attachment corroborates the content of the message and the dating of the photographs, and that the date of transmission rests on the client display alone until the message itself is examined." .
_:r14b11 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b11 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Warhol frame was transmitted. DSCF0020, exposed at 00:08:46 by the camera's clock, shows a copy of 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)' stood upright and facing the camera against the framed print. The documentation-photograph record already described this frame and read it as the earliest dated trace of the thinking that became The Auction as Artwork. The attachment establishes something further about it: the frame was not kept in the studio. It went to the auction house, inside the set of photographs attached to the first approach. The message says nothing about the book. So what is evidenced is that an art-historical reference was deliberately composed into documentation sent to Sotheby's, and left unremarked. That is a gesture made outward rather than a private note, which is more than the record could previously say. It remains short of a designation: the message frames the print as property being researched, not as material of a work, and the auction that the designation concerns did not yet exist in August 2008." .
_:r14b12 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b12 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Redaction and scope. Following this collection's convention, the Sotheby's specialist addressed in the message is identified by department and role only; their name and mailbox are withheld pending consent, as are the studio principal's then-current personal mailbox and telephone number, all of which are retained in the studio's private copy. The sothebys.com domain is deliberately retained, because the fact of an approach to that institution is the point of the record. The recipient institution may hold its own copy of this message; none has been requested at the date of this record. What this record establishes is an approach, an identity, and a set of photographs. It establishes no acceptance, valuation, or endorsement by Sotheby's, no consignor of record, and nothing about the 2007 origin asserted elsewhere in this collection." .
_:r14b13 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b13 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The thread continues to an executed contract. On 2009-03-18 the department sent a scanned consignment agreement, stating that a signed agreement was required before the photograph could be sold, and on 2009-03-19 the studio returned it signed as a PDF attachment. That file is held. It is described, checksummed and deliberately left unpublished in the consignment agreement record, which also sets out the two reasons it cannot be published: a confidentiality undertaking in the agreement itself, and a page of government identity documentation inside it." .
_:r14b14 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b14 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The business was disclosed at first contact, and what that does and does not carry. The opening message to the auction house, of 2008-08-14, introduces its writer, in its first two sentences and before it reaches the photograph, as an artist living in Eugene, Oregon and as 'the sole proprietor of a fledgeling Art business, Sun and Rain Works'. So the auction house was told at the outset, in the same message that raised the print, that it was dealing with a working art business and with the person who was that business. Three consequences follow, and they should be kept apart. First, the name Sun and Rain Works is attached to this engagement from its first minute, not applied to it afterwards. Second, because a sole proprietorship has no legal personality separate from its proprietor, the consignment agreement executed in the proprietor's own name seven months later is the agreement of that business, made in the only name in which such a business can contract; the earlier statement in this collection that the contract evidences an individual rather than a business drew a distinction this business form does not support, and has been corrected. Third, and against the grain of the first two, the message does not say that the business is consigning anything. No consignment existed on 2008-08-14: the auction house made its offer the following day and the studio accepted a month after that. What the message states about the property is personal ownership and research. The business is the writer's declared identity in the exchange, not a stated party to a transaction that had not yet been proposed." .
_:r14b15 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b15 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The estimate agrees with the published catalogue, and anyone can check it. In the offer of 2008-08-15 the cataloguer proposed a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000. The public catalogue entry for lot 16 of sale N08533, published some seven months later, carries an estimate of $7,000 to $10,000. That is a figure quoted in private correspondence before the catalogue existed, matching the figure the auction house later printed. It is the second point at which this studio-held correspondence can be tested against the public record without access to the archive, the first being the agreement of the verso markings in the 2008 photographs with the catalogue's physical description. The catalogue also prints the negative number 1-SF-46 and the American Trust Company commission, both of which appear in the studio's documentation." .
_:r14b16 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b16 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The outcome of the sale is not recorded anywhere in this collection, and should not be assumed. The public catalogue page for lot 16 shows that bidding is closed but places the result behind an account login, so whether the lot sold, at what price, or whether it was bought in or withdrawn, is not established by any source held or consulted here. Nothing in this collection asserts a result, and no reader should infer one. The studio has been advised to ask the auction house for written confirmation of the outcome as part of a records request; until that is answered, the sale result remains an open question of fact." .
_:r14b17 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b17 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "An earlier letter of the same week has since been produced, and it displaces part of what this record says about firsts. On 2008-08-11, three days before the approach to Sotheby's and under an identical subject line, the studio wrote to the photography department of the San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, repeating the same opening paragraph almost word for word. The earliest dated use of the name Sun and Rain Works in this collection is therefore that letter, not this one, and the first documented outreach about the photograph was to a museum rather than to the trade. This record remains the first approach to the auction house." .
_:r14b18 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b18 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b19 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The artist's account of the concealment, recorded as his account and given in 2026. Sun & Rain Works states that the work was conceived under the influence of the Merry Pranksters, and that disclosing a conceptual framing to the auction house would have destroyed the form: the counterparty's unawareness is, on this account, part of the work rather than a gap in it. The proposition is coherent and it is locally grounded, since Ken Kesey farmed at Pleasant Hill, some fifteen miles from the Eugene market where the studio was trading in the same years. It also disposes of an argument that appeared in earlier versions of these records: the absence of any art framing in eight messages of trade correspondence carries little weight, because that correspondence is with a cataloguer and an administrator about estimates, deadlines and signatures, and is not where such a framing would be put in any case. Two limits are recorded alongside it. The account is testimony given seventeen years after the events and is not contemporaneous evidence of what was intended in 2008 or 2009. And the model it invokes cuts both ways: the Pranksters concealed the prank from its subject while documenting it obsessively for themselves, so an appeal to that lineage explains the silence toward Sotheby's without explaining the silence in the studio's own contemporaneous papers, where the WTFIA folder of ninety-seven files, worked on until the week before the sale, never mentions the consignment. That internal absence remains the open question, and no account of the external one reaches it." .
_:r14b19 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b19 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status, stated plainly: all four messages are held only as screenshots of a mail client, and are therefore reported rather than verified. No message files have been examined, no Message-IDs or transmission records inspected, and no checksums published, so nothing here establishes by itself that the messages were sent on the dates their client displays. One partial exception is worth noting: the screenshot of the 2008-09-15 reply shows raw header lines rather than a rendered mail view, including a Date of 'Mon, 15 Sep 2008 13:56:41 -0400' and the sender and recipient addresses; that is a header as displayed, not a header as verified. The attachment is a different matter: the file is held and has been examined directly, and what follows keeps the two apart. The counterparty is an institution that may hold its own copies of this thread; none has been requested at the date of this record, and doing so is the single change that would most strengthen it." .
_:r14b2 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b2 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b20 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two further messages extend the thread through the autumn of 2008. On 2008-09-17, on a raw header reading 'Wed, 17 Sep 2008 09:45:03 -0400', the cataloguer writes that there is no rush, that the deadline is January 2009, that cataloguing for the April sale begins in November, and that she will put the consignor in touch with the department's administrator, whom she names, because a code is required for the item to be admitted to the building and unsolicited packages are refused. She adds a personal line about having holidayed in the islands when her family lived in California. On 2008-11-20 the consignor replies, opening 'Aloha Lauren', about the shipment of the photograph from San Francisco to New York for the spring sale, weighing whether to send it in its existing frame with the glass in place or to have a professional framer remove the glass to reduce the risk of damage in transit. He gives a post-office box on Maui as his mailing address. The header's weekday and its offset of four hours behind UTC, which is Eastern Daylight Time in September, are both correct." .
_:r14b20 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b20 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b21 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Two identifications the thread now settles. The administrator whose first name appears in the offer letter of 2008-08-15, and who chases the signed agreement in March 2009, is named in full by the cataloguer on 2008-09-17, so the same member of staff runs through the correspondence from the first offer to the executed contract. And the Maui post-office box given on 2008-11-20 is the Hawai'i mailing address that the auction-engagement record describes as linking the consignor to the studio's domain and hosting receipts. That link had rested on the studio's own account of its papers; it is now visible in a message to the auction house. The box number itself is withheld here, as personal address data, and retained in the studio's private copy." .
_:r14b21 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b21 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b22 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The register of the correspondence, weighed as a whole. The claim that this thread is purely commercial cannot be sustained in its strongest form. Across the eleven messages now known, and to three different recipients, the consignor's own language is persistently art-inflected: the museum letter of 2008-08-11 offers 'this happening' as something that might interest the institution; the approach to the auction house closes 'For the Love of Art'; the acceptance of 2008-09-16 capitalises 'the Art'; the message of 2008-11-20 speaks of 'the upcoming spring Art auction' and of 'this art in progress'; the covering note returning the signed agreement in March 2009 allows an exception to saving paper 'for the purposes of Art' and signs off 'Yours in Art'. That is six instances over seven months, in a consistent voice, and it is not the voice of a person disposing of an asset. To them should be added an act rather than a phrase: the signed consignment agreement was returned in a file named for the consignor with the word Art standing where his middle name belongs, which the consignment agreement record treats separately. What it is not, equally, is a designation. Every one of these is a turn of phrase inside a message about appraisal, shipping, glass removal or file formats, and 'art in progress' is at least as readable as the ordinary sense of an art matter currently underway as it is in the sense of a work being made. The honest position is that the correspondence carries a sustained artistic self-presentation and no statement that the auction is a work. Both halves of that should be reported by anyone citing this record." .
_:r14b22 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b22 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b23 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The Warhol frame, and a loop that closes inside the studio's own contemporaneous papers. The volume composed with the print in DSCF0020, and transmitted with the other ten frames to a museum on 2008-08-11 and to the auction house on 2008-08-14, is 'The Philosophy of Andy Warhol (From A to B and Back Again)'. That book is the source of the best-known statement of art as commerce: its Business Art section runs 'I wanted to be an art business man or a business artist. Being good in business is the most fascinating kind of art', and continues 'making money is art and working is art and good business is the best art'. Two things follow that are matters of record rather than interpretation. First, the letters carrying those photographs introduce their writer, in their second sentence, as the sole proprietor of an art business - the position Warhol's passage names. Second, five months later the studio compiled into 'IsItArt.doc' a press article quoting that same dictum in full. So the book was owned and deliberately staged in August 2008, the self-description in the covering letters matches its central idea, and the idea reappears in the studio's own research file in January 2009. The engagement with that specific thesis is documented across the period rather than asserted about it." .
_:r14b23 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b23 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b24 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the Warhol frame is, and what it still is not. The studio's account is that the book was placed as an obvious self-reference. The objective features support a deliberate gesture rather than incidental clutter: the volume is propped upright and square to the camera on the same ground as the print, in one frame of eleven, and the set was sent onward to two art institutions. Read against the transaction it accompanies, pairing the object being consigned to an auction house with the canonical text on art as business is precise rather than decorative, and the record of this collection identified that frame as the earliest dated trace of the thinking behind The Auction as Artwork before the studio said anything about it. Against that: the gesture was unremarked in every message, so nobody at either institution was invited to read it, and a cataloguer receiving eleven photographs of a print would reasonably see a book. Whether an unremarked gesture is a communication or a private signature is exactly the question the studio's account of concealment raises, and this record does not settle it. What it can say is that the gesture is deliberate, dated, transmitted, and thematically exact." .
_:r14b24 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b24 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b25 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P14_carried_out_by> <https://www.metaconceptualart.com/data/linked-art/sun-and-rain-works> .
_:r14b25 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P4_has_time-span> _:r14b26 .
_:r14b25 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E65_Creation> .
_:r14b25 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Compilation of the 2008 approach record" .
_:r14b26 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82a_begin_of_the_begin> "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r14b26 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P82b_end_of_the_end> "2026-12-31T23:59:59Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> .
_:r14b26 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E52_Time-Span> .
_:r14b26 <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "2026" .
_:r14b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The thread, message by message. 2008-08-14, studio to Sotheby's: the approach described above, with the zip attached. 2008-08-15, Sotheby's to studio, opening 'As per our recent conversation': an offer to include the Adams 'San Francisco from Twin Peaks' in the April 2009 sale at a provisional estimate of $7,000 to $10,000, 'bearing in mind that it is in perfect condition'; a deadline of the end of January 2009; an instruction that the print be arranged through the department before sending, since unsolicited packages are turned away; the first name of the administrator who would supply the forms; and an attachment of consignment charges. The phrase 'as per our recent conversation' records a telephone call between the two messages of which nothing else survives. 2008-09-15, Sotheby's to studio, on a header timestamped 13:56:41 -0400: a follow-up asking whether the owner still wishes to consign to the April 2009 auction. 2008-09-16, studio to Sotheby's: confirmation of interest in consigning, a note that the writer is in Hawai'i at that time, and an undertaking to arrange shipment personally." .
_:r14b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the auction house's own words settle. In the 2008-08-15 and 2008-09-15 messages Sotheby's names the object by the exact title under which it was later catalogued as lot 16 of sale N08533, values it, and proposes to sell it for the person it is writing to. That is the auction house, not the studio, attesting the consignment relationship. Taken with the documentation photographs, the verso agreement with the public catalogue entry, and the transmitted attachment, the consignment ceases to be a matter of the studio's account. Two further details follow from the thread. The writer's presence in Hawai'i in September 2008 agrees with the Hawai'i mailing address that links the consignor to the studio's domain records. And the sale was planned throughout this correspondence as an April 2009 sale, whereas N08533 was held on 30 March 2009 - a schedule that moved, and a fact that bears on the misdated month in the 2013 museum letter, which the correspondence record treats separately." .
_:r14b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "What the thread does not contain. Across six messages spanning seven months, in which the studio had every occasion to say so, nothing frames the consignment as an artwork, a performance, or a conceptual project. The studio's side is an owner's correspondence: research, deadlines, estimates, shipping. The auction house's side is ordinary trade. The two art-inflected touches are the capitalisation of 'the Art' in the closing message and the 'For the Love of Art' sign-off in the first, neither of which asserts anything. This should be weighed carefully rather than either way: the designation this collection records concerns the auction, and while the last two messages fall three weeks before the sale itself, they are administrative: a chase for a signed agreement, and a reply about post. An earlier version of this record said that not one message in the thread mentions art as anything other than the object being sold. That is now withdrawn as too strong: the message of 2008-11-20 describes the matter to the cataloguer as 'this art in progress'. See the note on the register of the correspondence below. The thread is therefore strong evidence of the consignment and of the studio's identity as a working art business, and it is not evidence that the engagement was framed as art at the time." .
_:r14b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b5 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The consignment agreement, March 2009. On 2009-03-11, nineteen days before the sale, the administrator of the Photographs department writes from a sothebys.com address, on a header timestamped 'Wed, 11 Mar 2009 12:01:52 -0400', under the subject 'in regards to consignment agreement for SOTHEBY'S'. She says the catalogue should have arrived and that she hopes the recipient is pleased with the presentation of 'your photograph'; that the sale is rapidly approaching and no signed consignment agreement has come back; that she posted it at the very beginning of February; and that she will issue a new one if needed. Her signature block carries the department's sale line, 'Photographs March 30, 2009'. The following day the studio replies that the consignment sheet was received but the catalogue was not, that the sheets will be faxed, and that post is reaching a general-delivery address in Eugene because forwarding to Maui has been slow. The administrator who writes here is the member of staff the 2008 offer letter said would supply the consignment forms, so the two ends of the thread name the same person." .
_:r14b6 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b6 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The consignor of record. This collection has until now recorded that the identity of the consignor was not established, on the ground that the published catalogue names no consignor, as is standard auction practice. That caveat can now be narrowed. In these two messages the auction house's own Photographs department, writing to the studio principal's mailbox nineteen days before the sale, calls the object 'your photograph', refers to 'your catalogue' and to 'your consignment agreement', and chases the signed agreement it had posted in February; the studio answers that it holds the consignment sheet and will return it. A consignment agreement is the instrument by which a consignor consigns. Two things should still be said. The signed agreement itself is not held and is not published here, so what is evidenced is the auction house treating this person as its consignor, rather than the executed contract. And the catalogue remains silent on the point, so a reader working only from the public record still cannot see it. That is why the statements elsewhere in this collection that the catalogue does not name Sun & Rain Works are correct, and they are left standing." .
_:r14b7 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b7 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Location, across the whole run of evidence. The 2008 opening message gives Eugene, Oregon. The market membership record places the same business at stalls in Eugene in 2005, 2006 and 2008. In September 2008 the studio writes from Hawai'i. In March 2009 it is receiving post at a general delivery address in Eugene while mail is being forwarded, slowly, to Maui. The Hawai'i mailing address that links the consignor to the studio's domain records is on Maui. These are four documents, two of them from third parties, describing one person moving between the same two places across four years, and they agree with each other. Personal addresses are withheld here." .
_:r14b8 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r14b8 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r14b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The attachment as held. 'San Francisco.zip', 3,553,357 bytes, SHA-256 227328da2ffe06ef140d79515499ddf1451a5669abaf20c9f9e70d384ed36c43. It contains twelve entries: the eleven documentation frames DSCF0012 to DSCF0017 and DSCF0020 to DSCF0024, and a Windows thumbnail cache. Frames 0018 and 0019 are absent, the same gap the documentation-photograph record notes in the studio's loose copies. Every one of the twelve is byte-for-byte identical to the corresponding file in the studio's private source archive, so the archive's copies and the transmitted copies are the same data. The archive carries Info-ZIP UNIX extra fields, header id 0x5855, holding access and modification times as Unix epoch integers; these decode to 2008-08-08 07:05:36Z through 07:10:24Z for the eleven frames. Those are unambiguous UTC values, unlike the local-time fields a zip normally carries, and they sit seven hours after the camera's own Exif clock, an offset equal to Pacific Daylight Time, the zone of the Eugene, Oregon address the message gives." .
_:r14b9 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
