_:r15b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Evidence status: studio-held, with self-describing file metadata. The dates given here are drawn from filesystem modification times and from the OLE document-summary metadata embedded in the Word files. They are internally consistent across the ninety-seven files in the folder, but they are reported by the files themselves and are not independently notarized; they should be read as the archive's own account of its dates. The press sources excerpted in 'IsItArt.doc' are independently verifiable at their published URLs. No checksums, file hashes, or custody identifiers are published at this time. The same storage medium also holds an unrelated 2009 website project on renewable energy, 'Growing Aloha'; it is outside the boundary of this evidence archive." .
_:r15b3 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r15b3 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r15b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "Embedded document metadata, as recorded by the files themselves: 'IsItArt.doc' gives author Joseph M. Chirum, created 2009-01-22T03:03, last saved 2009-01-28T21:23, revision 21, total editing time 09:46, 14 pages, 4,049 words, 23,082 characters; its Title field carries the string 'August 13, 2007', which is a metadata field value and not, by itself, evidence of a 2007 document. 'Photographers.doc' gives author Joseph M. Chirum, created 2009-01-22T04:19, last saved 2009-01-26T04:15, revision 4, 1 page, 69 words. 'OldMasters.doc' gives created 2009-01-23T18:22, last saved 2009-01-26T20:50, revision 19, 8 pages, 1,263 words. 'Modern.doc' gives created 2009-01-27T13:15, last saved 2009-01-28T03:49, revision 10, and was produced with a different word processor from the other three. A second contributor is recorded in the metadata of all four documents: as the named author of both dealer-network directories, 'OldMasters.doc' and 'Modern.doc', and as the last editor of all four files. That person's name is withheld from this public record pending their consent, and is retained in the studio's private copy; the attribution of the two directories is theirs, not the studio principal's." .
_:r15b4 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P2_has_type> <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416> .
_:r15b4 <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E33_Linguistic_Object> .
_:r15b5 <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P190_has_symbolic_content> "The folder is named for the 'WTF is Art' concept and holds the material Sun & Rain Works assembled for www.wtfisart.com: the art-market press compilation, the photographer note, and the two dealer-network directories of galleries and their web addresses that were built as the site's link content. Its art-market compilation is the documented source context for the image study Art Market Reform, and the dealer directories are a documented instance of the project's stated interest in an archaeology of the art world. The folder holds no HTML: the built pages of www.wtfisart.com are not among these files, so the folder evidences what was made for the site, not what the site published or when it went live. The folder also contains an Ansel Adams reference image dated 2009-01-21 and a photographer reference note dated 2009-01-25; these show that Ansel Adams and photographic authorship were present in the same 2009 working set, and nothing further. The 2009 Sotheby's consignment is evidenced separately, in the auction-engagement evidence record. One temporal alignment is worth recording: the latest-dated material in the folder - the modern-gallery directory, last saved 2009-03-23, and the seventeen art-market images, all dated 2009-03-23 - falls one week before the Sotheby's Photographs sale of 30 March 2009 at which lot 16 was offered. The folder was therefore in active use in the week before that sale. This places the work and the sale in the same period; it does not show that the material was made for the sale, and no such intent is claimed from the dates alone. Each item here is scoped to what it shows." .
